ne will want to be able to  access good pharmacogenomic information  readily available so as not to get lost  when the moment comes and the decision  to make a man change arrives a really  good online source is the Indiana  University department of medicine  clinical pharmacology table I've been  referring to this table since I started  residency back in 2004 it now goes by  the name of the Flockhart table probably  after some wealthy donor 15 years onward  and it is still going strong it's a  veritable classic if you choose not to  order any genetic testing at least make  sure you're aware of this and the  information contained here in this will  certainly help you navigate the often  complicated world  interactions between drugs substrates  and boosters and inhibitors that we see  in our practice daily I can  also give you many examples of how this  can come to play but here is one  scenario I'm sorry that this slide is  blurred but this is the best image I  could pull online a patient was being  treated with citalopram and bupropion  initially for depression when he didn't  respond was switched to fluoxetine and  bupropion then went to me for consoled  he was not doing well was feeling ill  and lethargic on initial visit what did  you think the issue was I wasn't sure  but I wasn't going to wait for a  serotonin syndrome or seizure nor was I  going to wait for  genetic testing to be done I went  straight away to get my Flockhart chart  the Flockhart chart showed me that both  bupropion and fluoxetine inhibited 2d6  five fold and fluoxetine was mainly  metabolized by 2d6  I could have ordered pharmacogenomic  testing but that would have taken too  long to for results to come I may have  found him to be a slow metabolizer to d6  and that would have been helpful but  either way this was something I decided  to act on independent of testing and I  decided to stop medication  the testing would have given me an extra  layer of information and it would have  been helpful but I decided to act anyway  another good online source of  information is the pharmgkb website this  website is terrific  it is an nih-funded resource that  provides information about how our human  genetic variation affects response to  medications they collect curate and  disseminate knowledge about clinically  actionable gene drug associations and  genotype phenotype relationships on this  website they have information on  individual genes drugs and drug dosing  guidelines all of which come very handy  this is the best and latest in  evidence-based practice another useful  resource is the clinical  pharmacogenetics implementation  consortium drug dosing guidelines the  clinical pharmacogenetics implementation  consortium or ce pick for short it's an  international consortium of individual  volunteers and a small dedicated staff  were interested in facilitating use of  pharmacogenetic tests for patient care a  barrier for implementation of  pharmacogenetic testing in the clinic is  the difficulty of translating genetic  laboratory tests to actionable  prescribing decisions for affected drugs  their goal to address this barrier is by  posting freely available peer-reviewed  evidence-based updatable and detailed  gene drug clinical practice guidelines  they seek to use systematized grading of  evidence and clinical recommendations  using standardized terminology using  peer reviews published and leading  journals and posted on their website  where it's regularly updated  cippec has a key guidelines to a bunch  of things pertinent to psychiatry are  the sea shift to the six variations of  stress atomoxetine h of a variations of  carbamazepine the sip to d6 and ship to  see nineteen variations of several  tricyclics including amitriptyline  remember mean desipramine imipramine try  mep amine and the same variations of  several SSRIs including escitalopram  fluvoxamine sertraline and paroxetine  now  you  you  why is this pertinent because this is  the South same information that you will  find on the FDA drug labels of the  aforementioned drugs particularly in  particularly in this section when they  discuss the pharmacogenetics of these  drugs and how these variances may affect  responses dosing  these guidelines rise to the sort of  scientific scrutiny that one would be  remiss not to know if you neglect this  information beware  conclude I think we all now understand  how important the community psychiatry  is being at the point of the spear of  the psychiatric pharmacogenomics  movement that is in play in our nation  there are more and more patients each  day who avail of these tests and it is  these practitioners in the community who  provide care to the patients who use  these tests the most there's also these  practitioners in these scenarios who  encounter the patients with the greatest  levels of treatment resistance and  side-effects  they are the bastion of hope and the  main bulwark against the community  stigma in psychiatric care we discover  that psychiatric pharmacogenomics can be  a tool in the armamentarium of the  community psychiatrists to cut through  that treatment resistance the mid side  effects and ultimately improve outcomes  for patients in the community who  experience unnecessary hardships brought  about by their off difficult-to-treat  conditions if used wisely of course as I  had mentioned there are limits to  implementation we learned that certain  genes and genotypes were relevant to  tests and there were basic resources on  pharmacogenomics and drug prescribing  information that are useful to end I  would like to thank my colleagues at lab  roots for organizing this and arranging  the logistics and for making this eme a  possible with that I thank you have a  good day and take care I would like now  to open the floor for questions  dr. penis thank you for that informative  presentation it is now time for the live  Q&amp;A if you do have a question you'd like  to ask dr. Benitez please do so now just  click on that ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen and we  will answer as many questions as we have  time for  okay dr. Benitez our first question is  what sort of qualities do you look for  in a pharmacogenetics test  Chris yeah I'll reveal my biases here  coming from an old-school first  generation approach where we used to do  all these tests individually rather than  how it's done  presently much rather go with testing  where I get a wealth of information  obtaining lots of genes from the patient  leaving me with a freedom to interpret  the information on my own afterwards  rather than going with tests that forced  me to think along the lines of  second-generation combinatorial  strategies which although may be helpful  if one is in a time crunch may be  ultimately limiting if one actually  wants to know what is truly going on  with a patient I honestly can't make any  specific recommendations about which  tests one can get in the market of  course because of the nature of this CME  but one should look to find tests which  will allow one to mine a plethora of  genetic information for their patients  and be alert as possible about these  genes to take the best advantage of the  available information that one can mine  thank you doctor Benitez now let's go  with this question and it's come in a  few times here what do you think of  tests like 23andme and ancestry those  are very interesting and fun  direct-to-consumer tests so in fact my  sister got one for herself and wanted me  to take one myself I told her thanks  this but no thing they are very popular  in this day and age but they do not give  you the necessary information to help  you if a patient asks you if it is going  to be helpful for them and from a  clinical standpoint particularly tell  them to refrain from getting one it is  not going to be helpful to them and it  won't help them make clinical decisions  related to care might help them if they  want to make pop quiz decisions or to  get information about their ancestry and  stuff along those lines but I would not  use that in the informed clinical  decision  thank you doctor Benitez now we're  almost out of time we'll wrap a disc  final question why have ensures or  managed care companies been reluctant to  pay for these pets well some have some  have not I think it boils down to  dollars and cents ultimately once more  research and evidence can be shown as  these tests have actually saving in  saving these companies money and save it  helping save lives reducing morbidity  and mortality and improving patient  outcomes along the way then these  insurance companies might recognize that  these tests might turn their heads and  recognize that these are very good value  propositions and well worth paying for i  I did come up with a study at one point  that showed that this was a value  proposition and several other studies  were were were shown to have proven that  and more more recognition of these tests  will will will prove that in the future  but if it's it's something that will  happen in time ultimately  thank you doctor Benitez now do you have  any final comments for our audience  oh yes actually I'd like to directly to  my references  here they are  a big thank you to dr. Joaquin Benitez  for his time today and his important  contributions to psychiatric  pharmacogenomics I would also like to  thank lab roots for hosting today's  educational webcast  before we go I would also like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration this webcast  can be viewed on demand and lab routes  will alert you via email when it's  available replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  and lastly one important reminder this  activity has been approved for one am  APR a category one credit if you are  interested in obtaining CMI's for this  webinar please complete the evaluation  and post-test that will appear here  shortly thank you for joining us today  and we hope to see you again soon  bye for now
 

